Ticket: SCRUM-5
Title: Add responsive mobile layout support to Vyasa UI
Description: ## Problem Statement

Mobile users visiting `
d2j5xbveesoc8s.cloudfront.net
` currently experience a desktop-only layout that renders poorly on screens below 768px. The sidebar occupies a fixed 264px width that consumes most of the viewport on mobile, the chat input suggestions overflow, and touch targets are below the recommended 44px minimum. This blocks adoption from users on phones and tablets who want to query the Mahabharata knowledge base on the go.  ## User Stories  - As a mobile user, I want the Vyasa chat interface to adapt to my screen size so that I can comfortably read and type messages on my phone. - As a mobile user, I want the session sidebar to appear as a slide-over drawer (not always visible) so that the chat area uses the full viewport width. - As a tablet user, I want the layout to gracefully transition between mobile and desktop behaviors at appropriate breakpoints. - As any user, I want touch targets (buttons, suggestion chips) to be at least 44×44px so that I can tap them accurately.  ## Acceptance Criteria  1. **Given** a viewport width < 768px **when** the app loads **then** the sidebar is hidden by default and can be opened via a hamburger/toggle button as a modal overlay or slide-over drawer. 2. **Given** a viewport width < 768px **when** the sidebar drawer is open **then** tapping outside the sidebar or selecting a session closes it automatically. 3. **Given** a mobile viewport **when** viewing the chat **then** message bubbles use `max-w-[90%]` and the chat input area spans the full width with appropriate padding. 4. **Given** a mobile viewport **when** the suggestion chips are displayed **then** they scroll horizontally or wrap to a maximum of 2 rows without overflowing the viewport. 5. **Given** any viewport **when** interactive elements (buttons, chips, textarea) are rendered **then** all touch targets meet the 44×44px minimum recommended size. 6. **Given** a viewport width ≥ 768px **when** the app loads **then** the current desktop layout with persistent sidebar is preserved (no regression). 7. **Given** a mobile viewport **when** the keyboard is open (soft keyboard on iOS/Android) **then** the chat input remains visible and the message area scrolls correctly without layout jump.  ## Edge Cases  1. What happens when the user rotates the device from portrait to landscape mid-conversation? (Layout should reflow without losing scroll position or input text.) 2. What happens when the virtual keyboard opens on iOS Safari where the viewport height changes dynamically? (Chat input must remain anchored and visible.) 3. What happens on very small screens (320px width, e.g., iPhone SE)? (All content must remain accessible, no horizontal scroll on the main layout.) 4. What happens when a very long message is displayed on mobile? (Word-break should prevent horizontal overflow; `whitespace-pre-wrap` must not cause horizontal scroll.) 5. What happens when the user pulls down to refresh on mobile? (Should not interfere with chat scroll behavior.)  ## Constraints  - Must use TailwindCSS utility classes exclusively — no CSS modules or styled-components (per Vyasa UI CLAUDE.md code conventions). - Must use Tailwind's default responsive breakpoints (`sm: 640px`, `md: 768px`, `lg: 1024px`) — no custom breakpoint configuration. - Must remain a React 18 functional-component-only architecture with hooks. - No new external dependencies for responsive behavior (no headless UI drawer libs) — implement with Tailwind + React state. - Must pass Lighthouse mobile accessibility audit (score ≥ 90). - Existing desktop UX must not regress — all current tests must continue to pass.  ## Affected Services  - `apps/vyasa-ui/` — All responsive changes live here. Key files:   - `src/App.tsx` — Add responsive sidebar logic (overlay on mobile, persistent on desktop)   - `src/components/SessionSidebar.tsx` — Convert to responsive drawer with backdrop overlay   - `src/components/ChatInput.tsx` — Adjust suggestion chips layout, touch target sizes   - `src/components/MessageBubble.tsx` — Adjust max-width for mobile breakpoints   - `src/components/ChatPage.tsx` — Ensure scroll behavior works with mobile keyboards   - `tailwind.config.js` — May need additional animation keyframes for drawer slide-in   - `index.html` — Verify viewport meta tag is properly configured  ## Out of Scope  - Native mobile app or PWA capabilities (service worker, offline mode, install prompts) - Tablet-specific multi-pane layouts (iPad split view) - Dark mode or theming changes - Performance optimizations for low-end mobile devices - Touch gestures (swipe to open sidebar, swipe to delete session) - Mobile-specific onboarding or tutorial screens - Changes to the `vyasa-rag-service` backend  ## Technical Considerations  - **Viewport meta**: Verify `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">` exists in `index.html`. - **CSS approach**: Use Tailwind responsive prefixes (`md:`, `lg:`) on existing classes. Sidebar becomes `fixed inset-0 z-50` on mobile with backdrop overlay. - **State management**: Add a `useMediaQuery` hook or use `window.matchMedia('(min-width: 768px)')` to programmatically control sidebar default state based on viewport. - **iOS Safari quirks**: Use `dvh` (dynamic viewport height) or `100svh` instead of `100vh` to handle the address bar. TailwindCSS 3.4+ supports `h-dvh`. - **Touch targets**: Audit all `<button>` elements — current send button is `w-10 h-10` (40px), needs to be at least `w-11 h-11` (44px) on mobile. - **Testing**: Add Cypress viewport tests at 375×667 (iPhone SE), 390×844 (iPhone 14), and 768×1024 (iPad). - **Animations**: Sidebar slide-in should use `transform: translateX()` for GPU-accelerated animation (60fps on mobile).  ## Open Questions  - Should the mobile sidebar drawer slide in from the left (matching desktop position) or from the bottom (common mobile pattern)? - Should suggestion chips be limited to 2 on mobile to reduce vertical space, or should all 4 display in a horizontally scrollable strip? - Is there a preference for handling the iOS Safari bottom safe area (home indicator)? Should we add `env(safe-area-inset-bottom)` padding? 

